<!--
  A feed declaring a NON-ENGLISH language (#2185 / S0.9).

  Every other RSS fixture in this directory declares <language>en-us</language>, so nothing in
  the tree could exercise the case S0.1a (#2172) states as its own acceptance: "a feed declaring
  <language>es-ES</language> persists language_raw: 'es-ES', language: 'es'". That acceptance was
  unmeetable until this file existed.

  It is a FIXTURE, not a language rollout: `es` stays enabled: false in config/languages.yaml, so
  an episode resolving to it is skipped with a reason (S0.8) rather than ingested. The point is
  that the language is PARSED, NORMALIZED and RECORDED correctly — es-ES -> es, with the raw tag
  preserved — not that we process Spanish yet.

  The regional subtag is deliberate. A bare `es` would pass a normalizer that only lowercases,
  which is the exact bug S0.2 fixed in Config._normalize_language: "en-US" -> "en-us" reads as
  NOT English at whisper_utils.py:50. es-ES makes the primary-subtag step load-bearing.
-->
<rss version="2.0"
     xmlns:itunes="http://www.itunes.com/dtds/podcast-1.0.dtd">
  <channel>
    <title>Sesiones de Sendero</title>
    <link>http://localhost/</link>
    <description>Conversaciones sobre construcción de senderos: drenaje, trazado, y las personas que construyen la ruta. Contraparte en español de Singletrack Sessions.</description>
    <language>es-ES</language>

    <itunes:author>Maya Koster</itunes:author>
    <itunes:explicit>no</itunes:explicit>
    <itunes:image href="/images/p10_cover.svg"/>

    <!--
      ONE ITEM, ADDED 2026-09-30 (V.6b).

      This channel carried NO <item> for its first two weeks, deliberately: it existed to
      exercise channel-level <language> parsing (S0.1a / #2172), which needs no episode, and
      test_fixture_mapping.py correctly rejected an item with no audio behind it.

      The item exists now because the audio and transcript do. `p10_e01` is the SPANISH
      counterpart of `p01_e01` — same show, same Maya, same Liam Verbeek, same two sponsor reads
      — so anything the pipeline does differently here is attributable to language and not to
      content. It is also the transcript every measurement in this arc was taken on (V.6a's
      hazards, S2.10's 174 requests, ADR-157's chars/token floor), which until now lived only in
      a session scratchpad.

      THE GUEST'S VOICE IS PITCH-SHIFTED, and FIXTURES_SPEC.md explains why: macOS ships exactly
      two Spanish voices and both are female, 11 Hz apart in median F0 against the English
      control pair's 98 Hz — close enough that a diarizer would merge them, leaving the episode
      with no guest and therefore no position-bearing insights. The shift reproduces the English
      fixture's geometry instead (host 169.6 Hz, guest 77.4, ad 89.6, against p01_e01's 176.4 /
      73.0 / 90.0).

      `es` is ENABLED as of 2026-09-30, so this feed is now one the pipeline processes.
    -->
    <item>
      <title>Episodio 1: Construyendo Senderos Que Duran (con Liam Verbeek)</title>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">p10_e01</guid>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Sep 2025 07:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>Maya conversa con el constructor de senderos Liam Verbeek sobre drenaje, trazado, y cómo mantener los senderos rodables durante el invierno.</description>
